# mpa

An MPEG-1 Audio decoding library.
https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/korandiz/mpa

## Strict CRC checking

By default the decoder ignores the `crc_check` field. Set `Decoder.CheckCRC` to
`true` to enable strict checking for CRC-protected frames. The setting survives
the automatic reset that happens when `Decoder.Input` changes.

Strict mode covers exactly the following:

* **Layer I, CRC-protected, non-free-format frames.** The CRC covers the last
  16 bits of the frame header and the actually coded bit allocation. The
  allocation shared by both channels above the joint-stereo `bound` is counted
  once.
* **Layer II, CRC-protected, non-free-format frames.** The CRC covers the last
  16 bits of the frame header, the actually coded bit allocation (shared
  joint-stereo allocation counted once), and the SCFSI fields of every
  allocated subband. SCFSI is taken from the actual channel fields, using the
  same allocation table and syntax as the normal decode path.

Scalefactors, quantized samples and ancillary data are not covered, and the
decoder never validates them as part of the CRC. Unprotected frames (the
`protection_bit` is set) are decoded along the original path regardless of
`CheckCRC`.

The CRC uses initial value `0xffff`, polynomial `0x8005`, MSB-first bit order,
and no final XOR. The `crc_check` field itself is skipped.

On a mismatch, `DecodeFrame` returns a `CRCError` with `Stored` and `Computed`
fields (recognizable with `errors.As`) only after the whole frame has been
consumed. The frame is discarded before it reaches the synthesis filterbank,
so it does not affect the decoding history, and the next call to `DecodeFrame`
starts exactly at the following frame. Ancillary data containing a fake
syncword is therefore never mistaken for a new frame.

Frames truncated in the middle still yield `io.ErrUnexpectedEOF`; underlying
I/O errors are passed through unchanged.

Strict mode refuses to guess the protected range of CRC-protected **Layer III**
frames and CRC-protected **free-format** Layer I/II frames, and returns
`ErrUnsupportedCRC` for them.
